[
  "Shot with a Canon EOS R5, 85mm f/1.8 lens. The dish is centered with ample negative space around it. The camera angle is a 3/4 overhead view (about 40°) capturing the full volume of two rustic, matte ceramic shallow bowls, with one bowl sharp in the foreground and the second bowl slightly blurred in the background. Shallow depth of field creates a soft, natural bokeh effect. Soft natural light streams from a window on the left, creating subtle, gentle shadows and a slight sheen on the olive oil-dressed ingredients. The scene is set on an aged, rustic oak wood table with visible grain and natural wear. Props include a casual wooden spoon resting against the side of the foreground bowl and a small, slightly rumpled linen napkin tucked underneath. A few scattered cherry tomato halves and a pinch of fresh parsley leaves are naturally strewn near the bowls. The bowls are filled with a hearty, colorful Mediterranean mixture: tender, nutty-brown farro kernels, chunks of pearly white chicken breast, and pale, tender quartered artichoke hearts. The halved, bright red cherry tomatoes provide vibrant color contrast, and bits of freshly chopped, vivid green flat-leaf parsley are tossed throughout. The mixture is lightly coated in a shimmering, glossy olive oil and lemon vinaigrette. The chicken is broken into natural, bite-sized rustic chunks sitting alongside the vegetables and grains, not stacked. Photorealistic, not AI-generated looking. The scene reflects a lived-in, warm, authentic home kitchen. Includes slight natural imperfections: an asymmetric garnish placement, a tiny trace of vinaigrette on the rim, and a few stray crumbs on the wooden surface. Warm, golden-hour color temperature. No plastic sheen, no perfect symmetry, no hands, no faces. Indistinguishable from a professional cookbook photograph."
]
